This implementation prevents buffer overflow by validating the source length against a maximum boundary before allocating memory. The buffer is allocated with proper size accounting for the null terminator, and the copied data is safely null-terminated.

if (src_len > MAX_BUFFER) {
    return ERROR_BUFFER_OVERFLOW;
}
char *buffer = malloc(src_len + 1);
if (!buffer) return ERROR_MALLOC;
memcpy(buffer, src, src_len);
buffer[src_len] = '\0';

After deallocating heap memory with free(), the pointer is immediately set to NULL to prevent any accidental use-after-free vulnerabilities. This pattern ensures that subsequent checks for NULL will catch any double-free attempts or invalid pointer reuse.

struct resource *res = allocate_resource();
if (res != NULL) {
    free(res->data);
    res->data = NULL;
}
free(res);
res = NULL;

Before allocating an array of structures on the heap, the count is validated to ensure it will not overflow when multiplied by the element size. This prevents a subtle integer overflow that could result in a small allocation followed by heap corruption.

if (count > MAX_ITEMS || count <= 0) {
    return ERROR_INVALID_COUNT;
}
size_t alloc_size = count * sizeof(struct node);
struct node *items = malloc(alloc_size);
if (!items) return ERROR_MALLOC;

Before multiplying image dimensions to compute total pixel count for allocation, an overflow check ensures the result does not exceed the maximum integer value. If multiplication would overflow, an exception is thrown instead of proceeding with a truncated allocation size.

if (width > INT_MAX / height) {
    throw std::overflow_error("Dimensions overflow");
}
int total = width * height;
if (total < 0) throw std::runtime_error("Invalid size");
uint8_t *pixels = new uint8_t[total];

Array indexing is guarded by explicit bounds checks to ensure the requested index is neither negative nor outside the allocated array size. This prevents out-of-bounds memory access regardless of where the index originates.

if (index < 0 || index >= array_size) {
    return ERROR_OUT_OF_BOUNDS;
}
int value = data_array[index];
process_value(value);
return SUCCESS;

A subprocess is launched using ProcessBuilder with a pre-split array of arguments rather than a shell command string, preventing shell injection attacks. An allowlist check ensures only approved commands can be executed, and the working directory is restricted to a sandbox.

List<String> cmd = Arrays.asList("tar", "-xf", filename);
if (!ALLOWED_COMMANDS.contains("tar")) {
    throw new SecurityException("Disallowed command");
}
ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder(cmd);
pb.directory(new File(sandbox));
Process p = pb.start();

Shared mutable state is protected by a mutex lock acquired via lock_guard, which enforces automatic lock release through RAII principles. All modifications to the shared data occur while the lock is held, preventing data races between concurrent threads.

#include <mutex>
std::mutex state_lock;
std::string config_data;
void update_config(const std::string& new_config) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(state_lock);
    config_data = new_config;
}

A file validity flag is checked atomically, and if set, a follow-up stat call re-validates the file's permissions before access, closing a time-of-check-time-of-use window. This re-check ensures no attacker can modify the file between the initial flag check and actual access.

#include <atomic>
std::atomic<bool> file_ok(false);
if (file_ok.load() && stat(fname, &st) == 0) {
    if (st.st_mode & S_IRUSR) return load_file(fname);
}
return default_value();

Message authentication codes are computed and verified using a constant-time comparison (MessageDigest.isEqual), protecting against timing side-channel attacks. The HMAC construction itself ensures both integrity and authenticity of the message without revealing partial matches to attackers.

import javax.crypto.Mac;
Mac mac = Mac.getInstance("HmacSHA256");
mac.init(secret_key);
byte[] expected = mac.doFinal(message);
if (!MessageDigest.isEqual(expected, provided_sig)) {
    throw new SecurityException("Bad signature");
}

Instead of using the unchecked operator[] for vector access, this code uses the .at() method, which throws an exception if the index is out of bounds. The exception is caught and handled gracefully, preventing undefined behavior and providing safe error recovery.

#include <vector>
std::vector<int> data;
try {
    int value = data.at(index);
    process_value(value);
} catch (const std::out_of_range&) {
    log_error("Invalid array index");
}
